# Generated text is normalised to LF so that outputs regenerated on Linux are
# byte-identical to outputs regenerated on Windows. Without this the
# reproducibility check would fail on line endings alone.
*.R        text eol=lf
*.qmd      text eol=lf
*.md       text eol=lf
*.yml      text eol=lf
*.csv      text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf
LICENSE    text eol=lf

# Figures are rendered by ggplot2 against whatever fonts and graphics devices
# are present, so they are not byte-reproducible across platforms. Tracked as
# binary and excluded from the CI comparison.
*.png      binary

# Last rule wins, so this override must stay at the bottom of the file.
#
# The pinned extract is content-addressed: R/load.R refuses to read it unless
# its SHA-256 matches the value recorded in data-raw/SOURCE.md. Git must
# therefore never rewrite a byte of it. The file arrives from the Stats NZ SDMX
# endpoint with CRLF line endings and the recorded hash is the hash of those
# bytes, so normalising them to LF on commit silently invalidates the hash on
# every platform that does not convert them back on checkout — which is exactly
# what happened, and what the CI check caught.
data-raw/** -text
